# The board bundle is generated output: `pnpm build:board` reproduces it from
# `packages/board/`. It is 177 lines of ~4,500 characters, so git's line-based
# merge lands every board change in the same handful of enormous lines — two
# branches touching entirely disjoint sources still collide there, and the
# resulting diff cannot be read, let alone resolved.
#
# `-merge` tells git not to blend the two versions. It keeps one side whole and
# reports the conflict, WITHOUT writing conflict markers into the file. The
# artifact therefore stays valid, buildable JavaScript through a conflict, and
# the resolution is to rebuild rather than to read:
#
#     git checkout --ours skills/plot/scripts/board/board-server.mjs   # either side
#     pnpm build:board
#     git add skills/plot/scripts/board/board-server.mjs
#
# Which side you keep cannot matter, because the rebuild overwrites it. Do not
# phrase the resolution as "take ours": under `git merge` ours is the branch
# being merged into, under `git rebase` it is the upstream, and this repo
# rebases routinely.
#
# This is an attribute, not a custom merge driver (`merge=rebuild`), on purpose.
# `.gitattributes` is versioned and travels with the repo; a driver DEFINITION
# lives in each clone's `git config`, so on CI and fresh clones the attribute
# would name a driver that does not exist and git would silently fall back to a
# normal merge. See docs/definition-of-done.md › Resolving a board artifact
# conflict.
#
# The CI no-diff gate is what keeps this honest: resolve by keeping a stale
# artifact and forget to rebuild, and the build fails.
skills/plot/scripts/board/board-server.mjs -merge
